# Generated by shipshape. This file is yours now.
#
# **The presentation kinds never hold a record, and an argument is how one gets in.**
#
# The call graph stops a shape or a component *naming* a record — `PersonRecord.find` in
# either is refused. It cannot stop `ProfileShape.new(person: PersonRecord.find(1))`, because
# at the shape there is no record anywhere in the source: there is a keyword called `person`,
# and nothing a cop reads says what it holds. Every rule about the presentation layer leaks
# through that one argument. Something holding a record lazily loads an association, writes
# through it, and reopens the database from the view — with `@person.orders` and no constant
# for anything to see.
#
# So it is checked here, where the answer is exact: the object is in hand and can simply be
# asked what it is.
#
# **`TypedArguments#typed` refuses a record too, and this is not that guard twice.** `typed`
# fires at the argument, where the message can name the keyword — but only for a class that
# calls it. This sweeps what the object ended up holding, typed or not, which is the backstop
# the first cannot be. One rule, checked where it can be checked and again where it must be.
#
#   class Shape
#     extend HoldsNoRecords
#   end
module HoldsNoRecords
  def new(*arguments, **keywords, &block)
    object = super
    refuse_persistence(object)
    object
  end

  private

  def refuse_persistence(object)
    object.instance_variables.each do |name|
      value = object.instance_variable_get(name)
      next unless Persistence.anywhere_in?(value)

      raise TypeError, "#{object.class}##{name} holds #{value.class}. This is the " \
                       "presentation layer — hand it the fields it needs, or a shape built " \
                       "from them, never the record."
    end
  end
end
